# DOS batch files must reach the working tree with CRLF endings on every
# platform and git config: MS-DOS 7.1 (Windows 98) COMMAND.COM - the field
# target and the CI's boot DOS - can fail to find goto labels in LF-only
# batch files, silently breaking the :logerr safety paths.
*.BAT eol=crlf
# ...and the lowercase spelling, because gitattributes patterns are CASE
# SENSITIVE on every platform (repo audit D6). Every batch file in the tree is
# `.BAT` today, so this pattern currently matches nothing - which is the point:
# the first `.bat` somebody adds would otherwise be checked out LF-only, with
# exactly the goto-label failure the rule above exists to prevent, and nothing
# would say so.
*.bat eol=crlf

# The same hazard, one layer up, and it was open for a long time: cmd.exe's
# label scanner has the same LF sensitivity, and scripts/build-driver.cmd,
# scripts/make-usbport-lib.cmd and test/run-host-tests.cmd all depend on
# goto/call labels for their failure paths. They are stored LF and were getting
# their working-tree CRLF only from core.autocrlf=true, so a clone with
# autocrlf=false or input silently lost it.
*.cmd eol=crlf

# The INF is parsed by Win98's 16-bit setup engine, which wants ANSI/CRLF.
# scripts/inf-gate/check-inf.ps1 already fails FILE-EOL on a bare LF, so this
# is belt and braces - but the gate catches it at build time and this catches
# it at checkout, which is the cheaper of the two places to find out.
*.inf eol=crlf

# src/sources is the DDK's build.exe input and is read by the same 16-bit-era
# tooling lineage. No measured failure, pinned for consistency with the rest of
# what the DDK build consumes.
sources eol=crlf

# src/xhci98.rc is compiled by the DDK's rc.exe, the same 16-bit-era tooling
# lineage as `sources` above, and every other file in src/ is CRLF. No measured
# failure - rc.exe accepted an LF-only file - so this is consistency rather than
# a hazard, pinned before the two conventions can diverge inside one directory.
*.rc eol=crlf

# The published text of a release - the readmes, the licence, the qualifier's
# notice and its linker map - is read on the target machine, in Windows 98's
# Notepad or DOS EDIT, and neither of those renders a lone LF as a line break:
# the whole file shows as one line. These were stored LF with no rule until the
# release-script review, and were reaching the working tree as CRLF only because this
# host's core.autocrlf is `true` - so a clone with `false` or `input` would
# have checked out a release directory, and assembled an upload asset from it,
# carrying LF-only text. Exactly the hazard the *.cmd rule above records
# fixing, one directory over. `xhci98.inf` in each flavour
# directory is already covered by *.inf, and the .sys/.EXE are detected as
# binary.
releases/**/*.txt eol=crlf
releases/**/*.TXT eol=crlf
releases/**/*.MAP eol=crlf
releases/**/LICENSE eol=crlf

# And the root LICENSE, because make-release.ps1 copies *this* file into every
# release directory: it is the source of the pinned copy above, so a pin on
# only the copy would leave the next release to be cut from an LF-only
# original. make-release.ps1 checks it as well, at the point of the copy.
LICENSE eol=crlf
